findings.description:
Line 140 says a `function` or `class` entry in `nodeIds` "is reported as such rather than silently filtered". It doesn't say:
- what the layer count then is (`nodeIds | length` including the stray entries, or excluding them);
- whether the run continues or stops;
- where the report goes (body gap marker, provenance, or console only).

An entry that resolves to no node at all also has no defined handling. Walkthrough step 1 maps such entries to `"UNRESOLVED"`, but the skill's behaviour isn't specified. The Risk Assessment (lines 439-445) relies on this check to make the correction safe on unmeasured graphs, so it needs a defined outcome.

Row 4's fallback covers only whole-section absence. 361 named "a node lacking `complexity`" as the most likely real gap (`361-slice...md:252-253`). The design doesn't say:
- whether such nodes are counted in the tier distribution;
- whether they get a per-node gap marker or a count;
- what "top ordinal tier" means when no node is `complex` (the highest tier observed, or always `complex`).

Lines 209-212 justify omitting `languageNotes` silently because it is optional, but `complexity` is a source field, so the "no third option" rule (line 186) needs an explicit answer here.
